N:Horselydown, by the southern end of what
N:is now Tower Bridge, was a large field anciently
N:used by the neighbouring inhabitants for
N:pasturing their horses and cattle, and was called
N:Horsedown or Horseydown. In the reign of Edward III
N:there were "three water-mills, three acres of land,
N:one acre of meadow, and twenty acres of pasture, at
N:Horsedowne in Southwark".
N:The famous 16thC painting here is also known as Fete at Horselydown
N:http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joris_Hoefnagel_Fete_at_Bermondsey_c_1569.png
W:The 1st Cu cross over the 2d Cu, and lead through to the top, and cast off .|
W:Then lead through the 3d Cu, and cast up and turn :|
W:Then single right and left, and turn each your partners quite round .|
W:then single right and left again, and turn each your partners quite round :|
